              "type": "text",
              "content": "Curiosity | Textbook of Science | Grade 6"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.257,
            0.087,
            0.585,
            0.113
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "12.2 Night Sky Watching"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.123,
            0.885,
            0.329
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "If it is a clear cloudless night, a large number of stars may be visible in the sky. If you stay in a big city, you may find that the sky is rarely clear and only a few stars are seen in the night sky. This is due to light pollution, smoke and dust. The presence of excessive artificial light at night time is referred to as light pollution. In villages or areas where there is less light pollution, a larger number of stars can be seen. Also, your house may be surrounded by tall buildings and trees, which may block your view. The night sky is best viewed from open dark areas."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.211,
            0.409,
            0.282,
            0.458
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 3,
              "bbox": [
                0.211,
                0.409,
                0.282,
                0.458
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_5_image_3.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.202,
            0.465,
            0.292,
            0.507
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Do y ou know?",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.256,
            0.359,
            0.885,
            0.492
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Light pollution is growing sharply globally. This is reducing our ability to enjoy and study objects in the night sky. Some dark sky reserves and parks have been established around the world. Light pollution in the reserves is controlled to preserve dark skies for research. There are organisations which are working to educate people about reducing light pollution."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.186,
            0.562,
            0.366,
            0.671
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Can we try to find any constellation or star of our choice on any night from our location?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.074,
            0.595,
            0.252,
            0.755
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 7,
              "bbox": [
                0.074,
                0.595,
                0.252,
                0.755
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_5_image_7.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.381,
            0.524,
            0.883,
            0.689
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Not all stars and constellations are visible from all places on Earth and on all nights in a year. For example, the Pole Star is not visible from the southern hemisphere of Earth. To identify a star or a constellation, you need to know how a particular constellation looks like and where to look for it in the night sky. To get familiar with the pattern of a constellation"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.688,
            0.883,
            0.792
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "you may use images such as Fig. 12.3 and Fig. 12.4. To find out when and in which portion of the sky a star or a constellation will be visible from your location, you may take the help of sky mapping apps that can be downloaded on a mobile phone, or other online resources."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.256,
            0.807,
            0.727,
            0.911
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Sky Map is a very handy app for identification of stars, constellations and planets from mobile phones. Stellarium is another such app. The computer version of Stellarium is free for download and has many features."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.774,
            0.792,
            0.843,
            0.843
          ],
